FADE IN:

EXT. QUAYSIDE - DAWN

Gulls wheel over a grey harbour. Fishing boats knock against the pilings.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
(quietly)
Every town on this coast keeps one secret. Ours kept two.

CONSTABLE HAROLD "HAL" BRISK, 40's, rain cape buttoned to the chin, strides the length of the quay. All six-foot-five of him.

CONSTABLE
Who left these nets across the steps?

FERRYMAN
Not me. I only carry people, Constable.

CONSTABLE
Then carry yourself off my quay.

INT. CHANDLERY - DAY

Coils of rope, tins of tar, a stove ticking in the corner. A brass plaque over the counter reads WICKHAM & SONS, FOUNDED 1888.

MARA OKAFOR (20's BLACK, CLOSE-CROPPED HAIR) counts coins into a ledger, lips moving.

MARA
Twelve shillings short. Again.

NARRATOR (O.S.)
Mara never trusted a ledger that balanced.

MARA
Who said that?

[[
EXT. CLIFF PATH - NIGHT
Possible extra scene here. Not written yet.
]]

EXT. SHINGLE BEACH - AFTERNOON

PIP, NED and TOBY, three boys of about twelve, race each other down to the waterline.

They each speak with a thick coastal accent.

PIP
Last one in buys the chips!

NED
You always say that and you never pay.

TOBY
I'm not going in. It's freezing.

.MUSIC CONTINUES

The boys wade in up to their knees, shrieking at the cold.

PIP
Told you it was fine!

NED
Liar!

NARRATOR (PRELAP)
That night, the lamp went dark.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE KITCHEN - NIGHT

WICK, 60's, the lighthouse keeper, wiry and salt-bleached, stirs a pot of something grey.

WICK
Lamp's out. First time in thirty years.

MARA
Then someone put it out.

WICK
(not looking up)
Or something.

> FADE OUT.
